w\" srcSet=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14749.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>96. Shri Avanti Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: A black-hued idol of Lord Shri Avanti Parshwanath, 37 inches tall and 30 inches wide, in the padmasana posture. Above the idol&#x27;s head is a canopy of seven serpent hoods.</p><p>Location: This temple is in the city of Ujjain, near the Kshipra River. The temple is 2,300 years old.</p><p>History: The ancient names of this city were Avantika, Pushkarani, and Vishala. It was the ancient capital of Malwa. During the reign of King Sudhanva, this city came to be called Ujjain. King Samprati, who, on the advice of Acharya Aryasuhastisuri, built 125,000 Jain temples, installed 12,500,000 (one crore twenty-five lakh) idols of Jain Tirthankaras, and renovated 36,000 Jain temples, brought great glory to this city. On an auspicious day, the lifespan of a fortunate divine soul in the Nalinigulma Vimana (a celestial abode) came to an end, and that soul descended into the womb of Sethani Bhadra. At that very time Mother Bhadra had an auspicious dream. As time passed, on an auspicious day, Sethani Bhadra gave birth to an exceptionally radiant son. The child was named Avantisukumal. Gradually, Avantisukumal grew up. Upon entering youth, he attained mastery in all 72 arts befitting a man; more than that, he also became accomplished in the art of dharma. He was married to 32 maidens as lovely as celestial nymphs.</p><p>Once, Acharya Aryasuhastisuri, while sanctifying the earth with his presence, came to Ujjain along with his disciples and stayed in the mansion school of Sethani Bhadra. During the night, the venerable acharya began reciting his study of the Nalinigulma Vimana chapter. At that time, Avantisukumal was seated in a nearby chamber, conversing playfully with his wives. The sound of the acharya&#x27;s recitation reached Avantisukumal&#x27;s ears, and he began to think, &quot;Ah! The venerable acharya is describing a scene... I have seen that scene somewhere before&quot; - and as he pondered this, he was instantly overcome with jatismaran gyan (the knowledge of recalling past lives). His previous birth appeared before him as though before his very eyes.</p><p>He realized, &quot;Ah! In my past life, I was a god in this very Nalinigulma Vimana. What a vast difference there is between the pleasures there and the pleasures here. The pleasures there are as vast as Mount Meru, while the pleasures here are as small as a mustard seed...&quot; (The page ends here, unfinished, and the story continues on the next page.)</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-116\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Kamitpuran Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-119\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Alaukik Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\"><p>१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन</p><p>Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur</p></div></footer><!--$--><!--/$--><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/40e0f9pufs6g4.js\" id=\"_R_\" asyn